plan:
My plan is to create a complete set of acceptance criteria for the Live Preview feature since openspec provided an empty array. I'll base the ACs on: (1) standard Contentstack Live Preview SDK behavior, (2) the identified attachment points (useContent hook, contentstack service, app bootstrap), and (3) the three-site scope (GO, UP, MX). I'll cover initialization, event-driven refresh, preview mode detection, graceful fallback, error handling, SSR compatibility, and data integrity. All ACs will be observable outcomes (Given/When/Then), not implementation instructions. I'll note that file contents were unavailable and my ACs may need refinement after reviewing actual code. My answer satisfies the requirement by omitting splitStories and providing acAddedBySpeckit for all new criteria.

```json
{
  "acceptanceCriteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-1",
      "criterion": "Given the application is bootstrapped, When a page loads inside the Contentstack Live Preview iframe or with Live Preview query parameters present, Then the Live Preview SDK is initialized and ready to receive preview events before any content is fetched."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-2",
      "criterion": "Given the application is running outside a Live Preview context (no preview iframe, no preview query parameters), When content is fetched, Then the application behaves identically to the current non-preview behavior — no errors, no missing data, no visible UI differences."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-3",
      "criterion": "Given a user edits content in Contentstack's Live Preview editor, When the Live Preview SDK emits a content update event for a content type and key that the current page subscribes to, Then the useContent hook returns the updated content data without a full page reload."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-4",
      "criterion": "Given a Live Preview content update event is received, When the updated content replaces the previous content, Then all components consuming that content via useContent re-render with the new data and reflect the author's changes in real time."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-5",
      "criterion": "Given the Live Preview SDK emits an event for a content type/key that is NOT currently subscribed to on the page, When the event is processed, Then no error is thrown and no unnecessary re-render occurs."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-6",
      "criterion": "Given the Live Preview SDK fails to initialize or throws a runtime error, When the application continues loading, Then content fetches still succeed using the standard (non-preview) code path and the page renders normally."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-7",
      "criterion": "Given a Live Preview content update event contains malformed or incomplete data, When the event is processed, Then the application does not crash, the previous valid content is retained or a graceful fallback is displayed, and an appropriate error is logged."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-8",
      "criterion": "Given the application is server-side rendered, When Live Preview SDK initialization is attempted, Then initialization is deferred or skipped during SSR and only executes on the client side, preventing hydration mismatches."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-9",
      "criterion": "Given a Live Preview content update event is received, When the useContent hook processes the update, Then the content data structure returned matches the same interface/shape as data fetched through the standard getContentByKey path, ensuring type consistency for consuming components."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-10",
      "criterion": "Given the user navigates away from a Live Preview page, When the component unmounts, Then any event listeners or subscriptions registered by the Live Preview integration are cleaned up to prevent memory leaks."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-11",
      "criterion": "Given the Live Preview SDK sends multiple rapid content update events for the same key, When these events arrive in quick succession, Then the application processes only the latest event's data (debouncing or coalescing), preventing stale intermediate renders."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-12",
      "criterion": "Given the Live Preview feature is deployed, When a component uses useContent with a content key, Then the component's behavior is unchanged for all existing non-preview consumers — no regression in content display, loading states, or error handling."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-13",
      "criterion": "Given the Live Preview query parameters or hash are present in the URL, When the application initializes, Then the preview content fetch uses the preview-specific endpoint or parameters provided by the Live Preview SDK, rather than the production content endpoint."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-14",
      "criterion": "Given the Live Preview integration is active, When content is served from preview, Then no preview-only data, internal Contentstack metadata, or draft content leaks into the production (non-preview) rendering path."
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-15",
      "criterion": "Given the three sites (gotransit, upexpress, metrolinx) each share the identified files (useContent.ts, contentstack.ts, contentCard.ts, ContentstackLink.tsx), When the Live Preview feature is implemented, Then all three sites exhibit identical Live Preview behavior — initialization, content refresh, and fallback — without site-specific divergence."
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Openspec provided empty acceptance criteria with useful context about attachment points (useContent hook, contentstack service, app bootstrap). I created all 15 ACs from scratch based on: (1) openspec's notes identifying useContent as the central hook and the bootstrap layer as the initialization point, (2) standard Contentstack Live Preview SDK behavior (event-driven content updates, iframe/query-param detection), (3) the three-site scope. Key areas covered: initialization (AC-1), non-preview fallback (AC-2), real-time content refresh (AC-3, AC-4), irrelevant event handling (AC-5), SDK error resilience (AC-6), malformed data handling (AC-7), SSR safety (AC-8), type consistency (AC-9), cleanup (AC-10), rapid-event debouncing (AC-11), non-regression (AC-12), preview routing (AC-13), data isolation (AC-14), cross-site consistency (AC-15). CAVEAT: File contents were unavailable during this review. The actual implementation of useContent.ts and contentstack.ts may reveal existing patterns (e.g., caching, error handling) that warrant additional or refined criteria. I recommend reviewing those files before finalizing.",
